      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-113",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In the example gevent WebSocket WSGI app, WebSocketWSGI.__call__ (itself a registered EntryPoint/handler, hop_distance=1, reachable_from_entry=true) builds the raw HTTP 101 handshake response by directly %-interpolating attacker-controlled request headers (environ.get('HTTP_ORIGIN'), environ.get('HTTP_HOST')) and the request path (ws.path) into a string containing literal \\r\\n sequences, with no CRLF/control-character stripping or validation, then writes the result straight to the client socket. A Host/Origin header or path containing CR/LF lets an attacker inject arbitrary extra response headers or split the HTTP response (classic CRLF/header injection leading to response splitting), something the core gunicorn WSGI Response.process_headers() path explicitly guards against via HEADER_RE validation but this example bypasses by building/sending the response manually.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read examples/websocket/gevent_websocket.py myself and confirmed the exact code: WebSocketWSGI.__call__ (line 37) builds `handshake_reply` by %-interpolating environ.get('HTTP_ORIGIN'), environ.get('HTTP_HOST'), and ws.path (= environ.get('PATH_INFO'), line 150) directly into string literals containing \\r\\n (lines 87-98 for the modern-protocol branch, 100-107 for the legacy branch), with zero stripping/validation of control characters anywhere in the file, then writes the raw result straight to the socket via sock.sendall(handshake_reply.encode()) at line 109 -- never going through gunicorn's WSGI header path. I independently queried the graph and confirmed __call__ is a registered EntryPoint of kind 'handler' and that every call in the taint chain (the %-ops at 87-98/102-107, the environ.get/ws.path accesses, and the sendall at 109) has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. I also read gunicorn/http/wsgi.py:247-259 and gunicorn/http/message.py:24 and confirmed the core framework really does validate header name/value via HEADER_RE/HEADER_VALUE_RE in Response.process_headers, which this example's manual socket write path bypasses entirely -- exactly as claimed. This is a genuine, unsanitized CRLF-injection sink reachable from a WSGI entry point. One caveat I could not fully verify myself: whether gunicorn's own request-line/header parser would itself strip/reject a raw bare CR or LF before it reaches environ (i.e., whether the HTTP/1.1 wire parsing layer provides an extra barrier) -- I did not trace that parser, so real-world exploitability via an actual HTTP client has some residual uncertainty, but the application-layer defect itself is real and precisely as described."
          },
