        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-113",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Same bug, duplicated in the eventlet-based example WebSocket WSGI app: WebSocketWSGI.__call__ (EntryPoint/handler, hop_distance=1, reachable_from_entry=true) concatenates environ.get('HTTP_ORIGIN'), environ.get('HTTP_HOST') and ws.path (all attacker-controlled HTTP request data) directly into the raw HTTP/1.1 101 handshake text with embedded \\r\\n, with no sanitization, and sends it unmodified via sock.sendall(handshake_reply.encode()). An attacker supplying CR/LF in the Host/Origin header or the request path can inject extra headers or split the response.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Source and graph both confirm the claim. In examples/websocket/websocket.py, WebSocketWSGI.__call__ (line 38, registered as an EntryPoint of kind 'handler', reachable_from_entry=true) builds the raw WebSocket handshake response text by directly %-formatting environ.get('HTTP_ORIGIN'), environ.get('HTTP_HOST') and ws.path (= environ.get('PATH_INFO')) into strings containing literal \\r\\n (lines 88-99 and 103-108), with no escaping/validation, and sends the result unmodified via sock.sendall(handshake_reply.encode()) at line 110. I additionally verified the attacker-controlled-CRLF premise against gunicorn's own HTTP parser: gunicorn/http/message.py's parse_headers() re-appends \"\\r\\n\" to each split line (line 67) and, when it encounters an obsolete line-folding continuation (a following line starting with space/tab, lines 99-106), appends that whole line (including its own embedded \\r\\n) into the header's value list before joining them with ''.join(value).rstrip() (line 106) \u2014 only the trailing \\r\\n is stripped, so an internal \\r\\n survives inside the final header string. gunicorn/http/wsgi.py then copies that value verbatim into environ['HTTP_HOST'] / environ['HTTP_ORIGIN'] (wsgi.py lines 120-139) with no character filtering (HEADER_RE in message.py is only applied to header *names*, line 93, never to values). So a client can send a Host or Origin header followed by an obs-folded continuation line to make environ.get('HTTP_HOST')/('HTTP_ORIGIN') literally contain an embedded \\r\\n, which then flows unsanitized into the raw handshake_reply text and out over the socket \u2014 a genuine CRLF/header-injection (HTTP response splitting) primitive. Graph query against scan_id 2b38b4ae18944de9f55caad2cf541fc6e8510713 confirms EntryPoint examples/websocket/websocket.py:<module>.WebSocketWSGI.__call__ (kind='handler', exposure='reachable') ENTERS_AT the method (line 38), and the CpgCall nodes at lines 88-110 (the %-format build and the final sock.sendall) all have reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, matching the analyst's citation exactly."
          },
          "locations": [
