        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "gunicorn's HTTP request parser (Message.set_body_reader in gunicorn/http/message.py) is missing the control that should reject ambiguous/invalid Transfer-Encoding framing. When iterating request headers it special-cases TRANSFER-ENCODING only to set chunked=True when value.lower() == \"chunked\" (lines 134-136); for any other Transfer-Encoding value (e.g. \"chunked, chunked\", \"identity\", a value with extra tokens, or any malformed value) there is no else branch raising InvalidHeader -- the header is silently treated as absent and parsing falls through to the Content-Length branch (line 140+). Likewise, when both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding: chunked are present on the same request, nothing rejects the ambiguous combination -- chunked unconditionally wins at line 139 and content_length is simply ignored, with no raise/error for the conflicting pair. A safe HTTP parser must either reject any Transfer-Encoding value that isn't exactly 'chunked' or reject requests that carry both headers, precisely to prevent a front-end proxy and gunicorn from disagreeing on request boundaries. This control is absent here, which is the same gap behind real-world gunicorn HTTP request-smuggling advisories (CVE-2024-1135-class issue). set_body_reader is confirmed reachable_from_entry=true with hop_distance=1 from an EntryPoint, i.e. it sits directly on the attacker-facing request-parsing path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against the real source. gunicorn/http/message.py `Message.set_body_reader` (lines 125-154) iterates request headers; the TRANSFER-ENCODING branch (lines 134-136) sets chunked=True only when value.lower()==\"chunked\" and has no else/raise for any other value (e.g. \"identity\", \"chunked, chunked\", garbage) \u2014 such values are silently ignored and parsing falls through to the Content-Length branch. Separately, line 138 `if chunked:` unconditionally wins over `elif content_length is not None` (line 140) with no check anywhere that rejects the case where BOTH Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding:chunked are present on the same request. Grepping the whole gunicorn/ tree for Transfer-Encoding/chunked handling confirms this is the only place inbound Transfer-Encoding is parsed (wsgi.py's chunked logic is for outbound responses, unrelated). Graph queries confirm this sits on the live attacker-facing path: Message.set_body_reader is itself registered as an EntryPoint (kind=handler, exposure=reachable), it's called from Message.__init__ at line 57, which Request.__init__ invokes via super().__init__() for every parsed HTTP request; the header-loop calls at lines 129-140 are reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. The installed version is gunicorn 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py: version_info = (21, 2, 0)). This exact gap (no validation of non-'chunked' Transfer-Encoding values, no rejection of simultaneous Content-Length+chunked) is the documented root cause of CVE-2024-1135, which was fixed in gunicorn 22.0.0 by adding exactly this validation to set_body_reader \u2014 21.2.0 predates that fix, so the target is in the vulnerable range. Note: the exploit_search tool returned the exploit corpus as \"not indexed\" in this environment, so I could not pull a live Metasploit module citation; the CVE/version comparison above is based on the patch history of this exact function, which I verified by inspecting the code gap directly, not by trusting the analyst."
          },
